    ├── Pitfalls   : requests.Session() vs httpx.Client(): constructor and 
    │   connection-pool semantics differ slightly; timeout semantics differ: requests 
    │   takes a single float/tuple per-request timeout, httpx.Client can take a Timeout 
    │   object with separate connect/read/write timeouts; exception types differ: 
    │   requests raises requests.exceptions.HTTPError/ConnectionError, httpx raises 
    │   httpx.HTTPStatusError/HTTPError hierarchy; httpx defaults to not raising on 
    │   4xx/5xx and requires explicit raise_for_status(), similar to requests but 
    │   response object types differ; aiohttp was suggested but rejected: it is 
    │   async-only, requiring the entire ScadaPoller class (read_tag/poll methods) to be
    │   rewritten with async/await, which is a fundamental architecture change beyond 
    │   simple import/call substitution
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── requests.Session() is used to issue synchronous GET requests with a timeout, 
        then raise_for_status()/json() are called on the response. httpx.Client() offers
        a very similar synchronous API surface (get, raise_for_status, json) and can be 
        used as a drop-in for the Session pattern without switching to async code, 
        matching the file's stated substitution target.

