Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading holding registers from a power meter
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, minimalmodbus, modbus-tk, umodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, 
    │   result.isError, result.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns a response object with .registers and .isError(); 
    │   minimalmodbus returns raw register lists and raises exceptions on error instead 
    │   of returning an error flag; pyModbusTCP's ModbusClient.read_holding_registers 
    │   returns a plain list (or None on failure) rather than a response object, 
    │   requiring different error-handling logic; modbus-tk uses a Master/Query pattern 
    │   (modbus_tcp.TcpMaster + execute()) that differs significantly from pymodbus's 
    │   client.read_holding_registers call signature; umodbus is primarily designed with
    │   server/framework integration in mind (WSGI/TCP server helpers) and its client 
    │   API and connection lifecycle differ substantially from pymodbus; unit/slave id 
    │   parameter naming and API differ across libraries (e.g., 'unit' vs 'slaveaddress'
    │   vs no such parameter); connection setup differs: pymodbus uses .connect(), 
    │   minimalmodbus configures serial/TCP mode in the constructor, pyModbusTCP uses 
    │   .open()
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All these libraries implement the Modbus protocol (TCP and/or RTU) and can act 
        as a client to read holding registers from a device, making them functionally 
        interchangeable at a high level. pymodbus is explicitly used here via 
        ModbusTcpClient with a response-object API (.isError()/.registers). The other 
        candidates are genuine Modbus client libraries capable of TCP communication and 
        holding-register reads, so they are plausible substitutes for the same 
        functional role.

