Equivalence Groups Found
└── finite-state machine engine for modeling process states/transitions
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat, pysm
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine, add_transition, model.state (dynamic trigger methods e.g. 
    │   start_fill/level_reached/trip)
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions dynamically injects trigger methods (start_fill, trip, 
    │   etc.) onto the model instance at runtime; python-statemachine/automat require 
    │   these to be declared as class-level State/Event/decorator constructs instead; 
    │   conditions='can_fill' and after='open_inlet' are resolved as string method names
    │   on the model — other libraries require callables or different binding 
    │   mechanisms; wildcard transitions ('*' -> FAULT) syntax is transitions-specific 
    │   and not directly supported the same way in automat or python-statemachine; 
    │   self.state attribute semantics (plain string in transitions) differ from State 
    │   objects/enums used in python-statemachine and automat; migrating would require 
    │   rewriting TankController's __init__ and machine wiring entirely, not just 
    │   swapping imports
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions provides a runtime Machine object that is attached to an arbitrary 
        model instance, dynamically injecting trigger methods (start_fill, 
        level_reached, start_drain, empty, trip) and a 'state' attribute based on 
        declarative add_transition() calls with conditions/after callbacks. 
        python-statemachine, automat, and pysm all serve the same conceptual role 
        (declarative state/transition management) and were flagged as plausible 
        alternatives, but each uses a fundamentally different architecture: 
        python-statemachine and automat require defining a dedicated StateMachine/class 
        with State() descriptors or decorators rather than dynamically patching an 
        existing model object, while pysm is closer in spirit (Machine/State/Event 
        objects) but still differs in API surface (event-driven vs conditions/after 
        strings). None of them is a drop-in replacement for the dynamic-model-injection 
        pattern used here.

