└── finite-state machine engine for defining states/transitions and driving state 
    changes
    ├── Candidates : transitions, python-statemachine, automat
    ├── Used APIs  : Machine(model=, states=, initial=), add_transition(trigger, source,
    │   dest, conditions=, after=), implicit state property (self.state), trigger 
    │   methods invoked dynamically (start_fill, level_reached, etc.)
    ├── Pitfalls   : transitions dynamically injects trigger methods (start_fill, trip, 
    │   etc.) and a `state` attribute onto the model instance at runtime; 
    │   python-statemachine and automat require states/transitions to be declared as 
    │   class-level descriptors/decorators, forcing a structural rewrite of 
    │   TankController rather than a drop-in import swap; conditions= and after= 
    │   callback wiring (referencing method names as strings) is transitions-specific; 
    │   other libraries use decorator-bound callables or different callback 
    │   registration; wildcard transitions ('*' source in trip transition) are supported
    │   differently or not at all in some alternatives; the file's own docstring 
    │   confirms this substitution requires re-encoding the whole machine, not just 
    │   changing an import
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── transitions provides a generic FSM engine that binds trigger methods and a 
        `state` attribute onto an arbitrary model object, driven by declarative 
        add_transition() calls with conditions/callbacks. python-statemachine and 
        automat serve the same functional role (declarative finite-state machines with 
        guarded transitions and lifecycle hooks) and could theoretically replace 
        transitions for this controller, but both use fundamentally different 
        declaration styles (class-based State/Transition descriptors for 
        python-statemachine; @MethodicalMachine.state/input/output decorators for 
        automat) rather than transitions' dynamic model-attachment + add_transition() 
        call pattern.

