└── Modbus TCP client for industrial field I/O (reading/writing holding registers)
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, 
    │   client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pyModbusTCP uses a different API: read_holding_registers returns a 
    │   list or None on error (no .isError() method), requiring rewritten error-handling
    │   logic; modbus-tk uses a Master/RtuMaster or TcpMaster object with execute() 
    │   calls and modbus_tk.defines constants instead of pymodbus's dedicated read/write
    │   methods, requiring significant call-site rewrites; unit/slave id parameter 
    │   naming and defaults differ across libraries (unit= vs slave_id= vs no explicit 
    │   parameter); response object shape differs: pymodbus returns a response object 
    │   with .registers and .isError(), others often return raw lists or raise 
    │   exceptions
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient to connect over TCP/IP and perform 
        read_holding_registers/write_register operations against a Modbus slave device, 
        checking .isError() on responses. pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both provide 
        TCP-based Modbus master/client implementations with comparable register 
        read/write primitives, making them plausible drop-in replacements for this 
        specific TCP use case (unlike minimalmodbus, which targets serial/RTU 
        communication, or umodbus, which is oriented toward building Modbus slave/server
        implementations rather than acting as a TCP client).

