└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing PLC/controller registers
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, pyModbusTCP, modbus-tk
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, 
    │   client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus uses `unit=` kwarg for slave/unit id in read/write calls; 
    │   pyModbusTCP uses a `unit_id` set on the client instance instead of per-call 
    │   kwarg; Error handling differs: pymodbus returns response objects with 
    │   `.isError()`, while pyModbusTCP returns None on failure and modbus-tk raises 
    │   exceptions instead; Register access differs: pymodbus exposes `.registers` list 
    │   on response, pyModbusTCP's `read_holding_registers` returns a list directly or 
    │   None; Connection lifecycle methods (`connect()`, host/port constructor args) 
    │   differ slightly in naming and defaults across libraries; modbus-tk requires 
    │   explicit TCP master/socket setup (RtuMaster/TcpMaster with hooks) rather than a 
    │   simple constructor+connect pattern
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus TCP client functionality capable of reading 
        holding registers and writing single registers over TCP/IP, which is the exact 
        operation performed in this file (ModbusIO reads/writes holding registers on a 
        host:port target). pyModbusTCP and modbus-tk both expose comparable client 
        classes for TCP master/client communication, making them plausible functional 
        substitutes for pymodbus's ModbusTcpClient in this context.

