└── HTTP client session for REST calls (GET/POST with JSON, timeout, status raising)
    ├── Candidates : requests, httpx, aiohttp
    ├── Used APIs  : Session, session.get, session.post, raise_for_status, 
    │   response.json, status_code
    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx timeout object/semantics differ slightly from requests 
    │   (httpx.Timeout vs float) though passing a float generally works; httpx exception
    │   hierarchy differs from requests (httpx.HTTPStatusError vs requests.HTTPError) - 
    │   raise_for_status() behavior is similar but exception types differ, requiring 
    │   exception-handling glue; aiohttp requires converting all methods (get_demand, 
    │   get_runtimes, command) to async/await and using aiohttp.ClientSession as an 
    │   async context manager - not a simple import swap; aiohttp response.json() is a 
    │   coroutine (await resp.json()) vs requests/httpx synchronous .json(); Session 
    │   lifecycle differs: aiohttp.ClientSession should be created/closed within an 
    │   event loop, unlike requests.Session which is synchronous and long-lived
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── The file uses requests.Session() injected into ScadaClient, calling .get/.post 
        with timeout, .raise_for_status(), and .json(). httpx.Client offers a near 
        drop-in sync API with .get/.post, .raise_for_status(), .json(), and similar 
        timeout semantics, making it a genuine substitute (as the docstring itself 
        notes). aiohttp is also a plausible functional alternative for HTTP client 
        duties but is async-first (ClientSession, async with, await resp.json()), which 
        does not match this file's fully synchronous call pattern without significant 
        rewriting of ScadaClient's methods to async/await.

