    ├── Pitfalls   : httpx.Client() is the sync equivalent of requests.Session(), not 
    │   httpx directly (httpx.get is a top-level shortcut, but the session object here 
    │   is 'Session'); Exception hierarchies differ: requests raises 
    │   requests.exceptions.HTTPError/ConnectionError vs 
    │   httpx.HTTPStatusError/httpx.RequestError; Timeout semantics differ slightly: 
    │   httpx timeout can be a Timeout object vs requests float; passing timeout=2.0 
    │   works similarly but edge-case behavior (connect vs read timeout) differs; 
    │   raise_for_status() exists on both but exception types differ, so any except 
    │   clauses referencing requests exceptions must be updated
    └── The file uses requests.Session() purely for synchronous GET/POST calls with 
        timeout, json body, raise_for_status, and status_code access. httpx.Client() 
        offers a nearly identical synchronous API surface 
        (get/post/json/raise_for_status/status_code) and can be substituted with mostly 
        a rename of Session->Client, matching the file's own docstring note that this is
        a substitution target.

